<ContentLayout structuredData={__sd} title="Logitech Brio 500 not detected on Windows 11: the real fix | StackGeist" description="A Logitech Brio 500 that does not appear in Windows 11 is usually a camera privacy setting, a USB connection or hub issue, an unpowered hub, or a software conflict. Diagnose in that order." active="guides">
  <article class="article-page">
    <p class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/guides">Guides</a> / Logitech Brio not detected Windows 11</p>
    <p class="page-kicker">Troubleshooting</p>
    <h1 class="page-title">Logitech Brio 500 not detected on Windows 11: the real fix.</h1>
    <p class="page-lead">A Logitech Brio 500 that does not show up in Windows 11 is usually a camera privacy setting, a USB connection or hub issue, outdated firmware, or a Windows driver problem. Diagnose in that order before you return it.</p>
    <div class="article-summary"><strong>The five-minute answer</strong><p>Go to Settings > Privacy & security > Camera and enable 'Camera access' and 'Let desktop apps access your camera'. Connect the Brio 500 directly to a compatible USB-C port on the computer. Open Logi Tune and install any available Brio 500 firmware update. Refresh the driver in Device Manager. Inspect the attached USB-C cable and test another USB-C port or computer.</p></div>
    <div class="article-body">
      <section><p class="page-kicker">Step 01</p><h2>Enable Camera access in Windows 11 privacy settings.</h2><p>Windows 11 ships with Camera access disabled by default for new accounts. A disabled setting blocks all camera detection and apps will not see the Brio at all. Go to Settings > Privacy & security > Camera. Enable 'Camera access' and 'Let desktop apps access your camera'. Restart the app you were testing in.</p><ul class="article-list"><li>Settings > Privacy & security > Camera.</li><li>Toggle 'Camera access' ON.</li><li>Toggle 'Let desktop apps access your camera' ON.</li></ul></section>

      <section><p class="page-kicker">Step 02</p><h2>Test a direct USB-C connection without a hub or adapter.</h2><p>The Logitech Brio 500 tops out at Full HD 1080p/30fps (or 720p/60fps). If Windows does not detect it, test the camera directly on a compatible USB-C connection and remove hubs or adapters from the path. Do not rely on USB port color alone to identify capability.</p><div class="note">The camera can become unreliable through a marginal or overloaded hub. An unpowered hub shared with a keyboard, mouse, and external drive may enumerate the camera but drop it under load. Test with a direct connection first.</div></section>

      <section><p class="page-kicker">Step 03</p><h2>Update firmware and settings with Logi Tune.</h2><p>Logitech recommends Logi Tune for Brio 500 firmware updates and camera controls such as field of view, image adjustment, and auto-framing. Install or update Logi Tune, connect the camera directly, and apply any offered firmware update.</p><div class="article-cards"><div><strong>Logi Tune</strong><p>Logitech’s supported Brio 500 utility for firmware updates, field of view, image adjustments, and auto-framing.</p></div><div><strong>Direct connection</strong><p>Keep hubs and adapters out of the path until the camera is detected reliably.</p></div><div><strong>Other Logitech apps</strong><p>Do not uninstall unrelated Logitech software unless you have evidence it is involved. Start with Logi Tune and the Windows camera/USB checks.</p></div></div></section>

      <section><p class="page-kicker">Step 04</p><h2>Refresh the driver in Device Manager.</h2><p>Open Device Manager, expand 'Cameras', and look for the Brio. If it appears with a yellow exclamation mark, right-click it and choose 'Uninstall device'. Check 'Delete the driver software for this device' if the option appears. Unplug the camera, reboot Windows, plug it back in. Windows will reinstall the UVC driver automatically.[2]</p></section>

      <section><p class="page-kicker">Step 05</p><h2>Inspect the attached USB-C cable and test another port or computer.</h2><p>The Brio 500 uses an attached USB-C cable, so there is no detachable camera cable to swap. Check the attached cable for damage and test a different USB-C port or another computer. If you normally use an adapter, dock, or hub, remove it during diagnosis.</p></section>

      <section><p class="page-kicker">Step 06</p><h2>Roll back a recent Windows Update if detection broke after it.</h2><p>Windows 11 has shipped UVC camera regressions in the past, most notably in early 2024. If the Brio stopped working immediately after a Windows Update, roll back that update and retest. Go to Settings > Windows Update > Update history > Uninstall updates. Select the most recent update, uninstall it, reboot, and plug the camera back in.[1]</p><ul class="article-list"><li>Record the date when detection stopped working.</li><li>Cross-reference with the Update history list.</li><li>Roll back only the most recent update — older updates are dependencies.</li></ul></section>

      <section><p class="page-kicker">Decision</p><h2>When the Brio still does not appear.</h2><p>Most detection failures resolve at Step 01, 02, or 03. If the camera works on a different Windows 11 machine, the original host's USB controller or chipset driver is the problem. If it does not work anywhere, the hardware has failed and warranty replacement is the next step. The video call guide below covers alternative cameras and setup.</p><div class="cards">
        <a class="card" href="/gear/video/logitech-brio-500"><h3>Logitech Brio 500 fit guide</h3><p>A 1080p webcam with auto-framing and light correction for a Windows or macOS desk.</p></a>
        <a class="card" href="/guides/better-video-calls"><h3>Better video calls</h3><p>The buying-side guide to cameras, microphones, lighting, and bandwidth for remote work.</p></a>
      </div></section>

      <section><p class="page-kicker">Sources</p><ol class="article-list">
        <li>[1] <a href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/camera-doesn-t-work-in-windows-32adb016-b29c-a928-0073-53d31da0dad5">Microsoft — Camera doesn't work in Windows (known issues and rollback steps)</a></li>
        <li>[2] <a href="https://support.logi.com/hc/en-us/articles/4406138714391-Getting-Started-Brio-500">Logitech — Getting Started with Brio 500 (driver and software guide)</a></li>
      <li>[3] <a href="https://www.logitech.com/en-us/shop/p/brio-500-webcam">Logitech — Brio 500 specifications (1080p/30fps, 720p/60fps, USB-C)</a></li>
      </ol></section>
    </div>
  </article>
